\begin{abstract}
Giving a language model a short skill document---a procedure, a lookup table, a
worked example---reliably improves accuracy, and a growing literature measures
by how much. What the document does inside the model is not known. We study this
directly, and find that the obvious internal measurements point the wrong way.
Over the layers in which a skill document is actually being read, the
displacement it causes in the residual stream is nearly parallel to the one
caused by an unrelated document of the same length (cosine $0.92$--$0.95$), so
displacement norms, cross-condition cosines and participation ratios are
dominated by the mere presence of a document. We therefore isolate the content
by subtraction, $\dvec = h_{\text{skill}} - h_{\text{wrong-skill}}$, against the
presence component $\gvec = h_{\text{wrong-skill}} - h_{\text{no-doc}}$, and
test both causally. The result inverts the geometry: $\gvec$, the larger of the
two over most of the network, repairs zero items at every layer---and faithfully
reproduces the behaviour of having the wrong document in the prompt---while
$\dvec$ repairs $0.81$ of the items the document repairs. The content component
is low-dimensional (effective rank 3--8 out of 2048 once its shared direction is
removed), it partly cancels the presence displacement---through layer 16 the
\emph{wrong} document moves the state further than the right one---and its
direction identifies the skill: on 55 third-party clinical-calculator skills it
supports leave-one-out identification of \emph{which} of 41 skills is in the prompt at
$0.93$--$1.00$ against a chance rate of $0.024$, while the presence component
scores below the no-document baseline. It does so at layers where injecting it
repairs nothing, so on that material the content is demonstrably present and
demonstrably not transportable. Three of our results
are cautionary and quantitative. First, how much of a document an activation
patch can carry is set by how long the answer is. Holding the model, the
document and the items fixed and changing only the answer format, a
single-position patch recovers $1.000$ of the rescued items when the answer is
one option letter, $0.304$ when it is a two- to four-token number, and
$\mathbf{0.000}$ when the same items are answered by reasoning step by
step---a format in which the document itself is worth $0 \to 1$. The clinical
results follow the same rule rather than forming an exception to it. Second, in multiple choice a
document with every number replaced retains two thirds of the skill effect,
while in free-form it retains none. Third, an aggregate gold-answer
log-probability ranks a content-free control above the true state, and the
entire gap comes from items where the skill does not work.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A ``skill'' is a short document---a procedure, a formula, a lookup table, a
worked example---placed in a model's context so it can do a task it otherwise
cannot. The practice is now standard, and there is a benchmark literature that
measures its effect: how much accuracy a skill buys, which module of the
document matters, how the gain scales. Every one of those measurements is
behavioural. None of them says what the document does inside the model.

The natural way to look is to compare the model's internal state with and
without the document. We did, and the first thing we found was that this
comparison does not work in its obvious form. At the last prompt position, the
residual stream moves a long way when a skill document is added---and through
the layers in which the document is actually being read it moves almost exactly
as far, in almost the same direction, when the document is replaced by an
unrelated one of the same length. On our synthetic control the cosine between
the two displacements is $0.92$--$0.95$ from layer 0 to layer 10, peaking at
$0.9475$ (CI$_{95}$ $[0.9469, 0.9479]$) at layer 4. On a second task family, at
layer 10, two skills with mutually exclusive content sit at $0.9716$ while an
\emph{irrelevant} document sits at $0.9445$ and $0.9555$ from them---a
difference of $0.016$--$0.027$, tightly estimated and almost nothing. Read
naively, that says the injection carries little information about what the
document says. Read against behaviour, it cannot: on 1{,}100
clinical-calculator instances with third-party skills, the gold skill is worth
$+40.5$pp over no document, and a length-matched \emph{wrong} skill is worth
$-1.3$pp $[-7.6, +5.7]$---a clean zero. Behaviour is all content. Geometry, over
the layers that do the reading, is almost all presence.

