Three facts in Figure~\ref{fig:decomp} are worth stating separately; the
numbers are in Table~\ref{tab:geometry}, Appendix~\ref{app:tables}.

\textbf{(i) The content component is a minority of the displacement where the
document is being read.} Through layer 14 it is under half of $\|\tvec\|$. The
ratio reaches $1.0$ deeper, but not because $\gvec$ shrinks---it is because
$\dvec$ and $\gvec$ partly cancel.

\textbf{(ii) The wrong document moves the state further than the right one, and
the two components partly cancel.} Through layer 16, $\|\gvec\| > \|\tvec\|$: an
irrelevant document of matched length displaces the final prompt position
\emph{more} than the skill does. The reported $\cos(\dvec,\gvec) \in
[-0.60,-0.32]$ at every layer is the law-of-cosines restatement of that fact and
not independent evidence for it---given the three norms it is determined, and we
verify that it agrees with $(\|\tvec\|^2 - \|\gvec\|^2 -
\|\dvec\|^2)/(2\|\gvec\|\|\dvec\|)$ to within $0.002$ at all 28 layers. What is
substantive is the cancellation itself: the content component's contribution is
partly to undo a displacement that any document produces. The causal test in
\S\ref{sec:decomp} agrees---injecting $\gvec$ alone drives accuracy below the
no-document baseline, to the level of actually inserting the wrong document. The
two norms cross at layer 17, which is also the first layer at which injecting
$\dvec$ alone repairs anything (Table~\ref{tab:causal}).

\textbf{(iii) The content component is low-dimensional.} After removing the
shared mean direction, the item-to-item variation of $\{\dvec_i\}$ occupies an
effective 3--8 dimensions out of 2048, and 87--99.7\% of each item's $\dvec_i$
lies along the shared direction. The content component is therefore well
described as one direction plus a small, low-dimensional per-item correction---a
description that \S\ref{sec:whose} turns into a testable claim about what the
direction belongs to.

\section{Which component does the work}
\label{sec:decomp}

We add each component back to the no-document state at the last prompt position
and let the forward pass continue: $h' = h_{\text{no}} + \alpha v$ for
$v \in \{\dvec_i, \gvec_i, \tvec_i, \bar{\dvec}, \dvec_j\}$. The upper reference
is replacing the state outright with $h_{\text{skill}}$; the lower reference is
no intervention.


