So the three-window picture is a multiple-choice phenomenon. When the answer has
to be produced rather than selected, the content is not relocated into any
compact carrier: it stays where it was written and is consumed from there. Each
window transports the model's \emph{decision} as soon as the decision has been
made, and a decision is one token only in multiple choice.

\paragraph{Transplants prove sufficiency; only knockouts prove necessity.}
Masking attention from every position to the document from layer 0 onward costs
the entire benefit ($0.436 \to 0.205$, against a no-document baseline of
$0.231$). But masking only the final position's attention to the document costs
\emph{nothing} ($0.436$), and masking only the question span's leaves $0.282$.
No single pathway is necessary: the read is redundantly distributed---which is
what \citet{jit2025taskrepr} report for task representations generally.
Separately, masking from layer 22 onward is free on all five knockout arms,
placing the last layer at which the model still needs to read the document at
21---the layer at which the final-position transplant becomes item-specific
(\S\ref{sec:whose}), at which another item's content vector stops working, and
at which the parallel and orthogonal halves of $\dvec$ swap roles
(Table~\ref{tab:parperp}). \citet{onset2025instruction} find an analogous
``onset'' layer from patching rather than knockout.

\paragraph{The same knockout on third-party skills.} Making the document's
tokens unattendable for the whole forward, and decoding the full chain of
thought, takes accuracy from $0.550$ $[0.400, 0.700]$ to $0.075$
$[0.000, 0.175]$ on 40 clinical-calculator instances---against $0.025$
$[0.000, 0.075]$ with no document in the prompt at all. Ninety per cent of the
benefit is gone, and the remainder is inside the no-document interval. Masking an
equal-length span of the patient note instead (median 893 tokens against the
skill's 919) costs less: $0.150$ $[0.050, 0.275]$. A length-matched control that
is genuinely inert cannot be built on this material---the only other long span
is the data the task needs---so this bounds ``removing 900 tokens'' loosely, but
it bounds it in the right direction.

\paragraph{And it keeps reading much longer.} Blocking attention to the document
cumulatively from layer $L$ to the end costs the entire benefit for every $L$ up
to 18, and still costs $0.500 \to 0.300$ from layer 30 of 36
($n=20$; $0.050$ from layers 0, 6, 12 and 18, $0.100$ from 24). On the synthetic
multiple-choice task the same intervention is \emph{free} from layer 22 of 28 on
all five knockout arms. The document stops being needed early when the answer is
one token and is still being read at $83\%$ depth when the answer is several
hundred---the same principle as \S\ref{sec:onepos}, seen from the necessity
side.

We therefore state the windows as \emph{where the content has accumulated}, not
as a route. Single-layer knockouts measure nothing on a 688-token span, because
a layer denied the read recovers it one layer later; cumulative knockouts are
required, and are what we report.

\section{What the content component belongs to}
\label{sec:whose}

If $\dvec$ were a ``skill vector'' it would be reusable: extracted from one task
it would work on another. Its \emph{direction} is reusable. Its \emph{effect}
is not, and the gap between those two statements is the finding.

\paragraph{The direction identifies the skill.} On the clinical-calculator
material each skill has 20 instances, so ``same skill, different task'' is
directly measurable. Content vectors from different patient notes under the
same calculator have cosine $0.789$ at layer 20 and $0.876$ at layer 30;
vectors from different calculators have $0.320$ and $0.653$ ($n=40$, 20 rescued
and 20 persistent, Qwen3-8B). The same measurement taken at the end of a prompt
that forces an immediate answer gives $0.793$ against $0.209$, so the gap is
robust to where the state is read but its size is not. On the synthetic tier,
which has a single document, the analogous split is by conversion family:
$0.859$ within against $0.699$ across at the last layer, rising to $0.99$ for
both in early layers. So the direction of $\dvec$ carries the identity of the
document, and increasingly so with depth.

\paragraph{The effect does not transfer at all.} Injecting the mean content
direction $\bar{\dvec}$, or another item's $\dvec_j$, repairs
\emph{nothing} in the free-form format---$0.000$ at every one of 16 layers for
both, against $0.268$ for the item's own $\dvec_i$ (Table~\ref{tab:onepos}). In
multiple choice the picture is graded rather than flat but still far from the
item's own vector: at layer 27, $\bar{\dvec}$ reaches $0.062$, a same-family
donor $0.292$ and a different-family donor $0.167$, against $0.792$ for
$\dvec_i$ (Table~\ref{tab:causal}). The one band where a donor vector looks
genuinely transferable---$0.33$ at layers 18--20, close to $\dvec_i$'s
$0.40$---is the band we identify independently in \S\ref{sec:traps} as one where
the forward pass is damaged and every condition rises together.

\paragraph{And nothing about it distinguishes rescued items from persistent
failures.} It is natural to hope that the items a skill rescues differ, at the
final position, from the ones it does not. Two independent measurements say
they do not. The norms and angles of $\dvec$ and $\gvec$ are the same in both
cells to three digits (at layer 30 of the 8B on real skills: $\|\dvec\|$ $189.4$
versus $185.4$, $\|\gvec\|$ $213.1$ versus $205.2$, $\cos(\dvec,\gvec)$ $-0.469$
versus $-0.468$). And a representational-similarity measure over the question
span, which does separate the two cells under the gold skill at layers 10--16
($-0.0037$ to $-0.0070$, non-overlapping bootstrap CIs, $n=150+150$), separates
them just as much under a length-matched \emph{wrong} skill ($-0.0035$ to
$-0.0056$): zero layers pass a control the design required in advance. What
looked like a signature of ``the skill worked here'' is a signature of ``a
document is present''---the same trap as \S\ref{sec:geometry}, one register
down, and the reason we report the causal channel rather than a similarity
score.

\paragraph{Decodable is not usable.} The clinical material has 55 skills with
20 instances each, which makes ``does this vector encode which skill is
present'' an estimable question rather than a description. We answer it with
leave-one-out nearest-class-mean classification of the calculator id on cosine
similarity---no training, no hyperparameter---over 287 instances and 41
calculators, chance $0.024$ (Table~\ref{tab:decode} and
Figure~\ref{fig:decode}).

